power: supply: bq2429x: only apply battery currents the device tree gives

bq2429x_parse_dt() reads two optional properties from the monitored
battery node, and each test ends in a stray semicolon:

	if (!of_property_read_u32(battery_np,
			"precharge-current-microamp",
			&value));
		bq2429x_set_precharge_current_uA(di, value);

so the if has an empty body and both setters run unconditionally.  When a
property is missing, of_property_read_u32() leaves value untouched, and
value is an uninitialised local, so whatever was on the stack is
converted and written into REG03 (pre-charge and termination current).

The Pyra hits this on every probe: its battery node has neither
property.  bq2429x_init_registers() later in the same probe writes
128 mA into both fields, which hides it, but only if the probe gets that
far; a failure or deferral in between leaves the charger running with
the garbage settings until the next probe.

Drop the semicolons so each setter runs only when its property was read.

jedi23 found this and made the same change in his Pyra M4 kernel
(https://github.com/micha2358/Pyra-Kernel, published by 2026-10-21).
This patch is taken from there.
---
--- a/drivers/power/supply/bq2429x_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/bq2429x_charger.c
@@ -1525,11 +1525,11 @@
 				&di->chg_current_uA);
 		if (!of_property_read_u32(battery_np,
 				"precharge-current-microamp",
-				&value));
+				&value))
 			bq2429x_set_precharge_current_uA(di, value);
 		if (!of_property_read_u32(battery_np,
 				"charge-term-current-microamp",
-				&value));
+				&value))
 			bq2429x_set_charge_term_current_uA(di, value);
 		of_node_put(battery_np);
 	}
